\chapter{User Codecs, Abstract I/\allowbreak{}O and Static Binary Streams}

The user defines data syntax and backend operations. MuDSL provides protocols, template forwarding, byte-stream helpers and static byte matching.

\section{Serializer and deserializer protocols}

\texttt{S\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}>} /\allowbreak{} \texttt{S\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d} borrows a backend with \texttt{s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})}. \texttt{D\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r} borrows one with \texttt{d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} in C++ or \texttt{d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} in D. Input/\allowbreak{}output types and operation results are chosen by the backend; they need not be I/\allowbreak{}O adapters or binary streams.

The following user codec chooses a four-byte, big-endian integer syntax.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <cassert>

struct IntegerCodec {
    void serialize(const int& value, dsl::BinaryStream& output) {
        output.write_integer(static_cast<std::uint32_t>(value), dsl::ByteOrder::Big);
    }
    template<class T> T deserialize(dsl::BinaryStream& input) {
        return static_cast<T>(input.read_integer<std::uint32_t>(dsl::ByteOrder::Big));
    }
};

int main() {
    IntegerCodec codec;
    dsl::Serializer serializer(codec);
    dsl::Deserializer deserializer(codec);
    dsl::BinaryStream encoded;
    serializer.serialize(42, encoded);
    assert(deserializer.deserialize<int>(encoded) == 42);
}
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
struct IntegerCodec
{
    void serialize(int value, ref BinaryStream output)
    { output.write_integer(cast(uint) value, ByteOrder.Big); }
    T deserialize(T)(ref BinaryStream input)
    { return cast(T) input.read_integer!uint(ByteOrder.Big); }
}

IntegerCodec codec;
auto serializer = Serializer!IntegerCodec(codec);
auto deserializer = Deserializer!IntegerCodec(codec);
BinaryStream encoded;
serializer.serialize(42, encoded);
assert(deserializer.deserialize!int(encoded) == 42);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

C++ protocol queries are \texttt{S\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}>} and \texttt{D\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}>} concepts. D uses the corresponding \texttt{S\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} and \texttt{D\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} templates. A backend can return its own result/\allowbreak{}error object; forwarding preserves the return type. The adapter does not add a wire format or schema.

