\texttt{I\allowbreak{}O} borrows its backend. In C++ it stores a reference; in D the constructor takes a \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f} backend and stores a pointer. Keep the backend alive and at a stable location for the adapter's use. The backend owns its resource policy.

Here is a user-defined sequential memory backend in D:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
struct MemoryBackend
{
    ubyte[] data;
    size_t pos;
    size_t read(ubyte[] target)
    {
        auto available = data.length - pos;
        auto n = target.length < available ? target.length : available;
        target[0 .. n] = data[pos .. pos + n];
        pos += n;
        return n;
    }
    size_t write(const(ubyte)[] source)
    { data ~= source; return source.length; }
}

MemoryBackend backend;
auto io = IO!MemoryBackend(backend);
ubyte[] payload = [0, 255, 65];
io.write_all(payload);
auto stream = BinaryStream.load(io);
assert(stream.bytes == payload);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The C++ form substitutes a vector, spans and \texttt{I\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{})}. A user can equally supply a file, socket, device or callback backend with this protocol.

\subsection{Transfers and optional operations}

\texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d}/\allowbreak{}\texttt{w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e} validate that a backend's count does not exceed its buffer. Short transfers are permitted. Zero read denotes EOF. \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t} fills an entire buffer or raises an exception on premature EOF; \texttt{w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}l} completes all output or raises an exception when a write makes no progress. Backend exceptions propagate.

\texttt{s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{})} and \texttt{t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} are available when the backend implements them. The core read/\allowbreak{}write protocol does not require random access. The example above therefore has neither operation.

\section{Static byte regular expressions}

\texttt{S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}>} in C++ and \texttt{S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e} in D compile a Thompson NFA from a string template argument. Invalid supported-syntax patterns are diagnosed at compilation. Matching uses byte values, so NUL, newlines and bytes from \texttt{0\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}8\allowbreak{}0} through \texttt{0\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}f} are ordinary input values.

