The current engine does not supply captures, counted repetitions, backreferences or shorthand classes such as \texttt{\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}d}. Use \texttt{[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]} for digits.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
assert(dsl::StaticRegex<"^(ab|a)+[0-9]?$">::matches("aba7"));
std::vector<std::byte> bytes{std::byte{0}, std::byte{255}, std::byte{'A'}};
assert(dsl::StaticRegex<"\\x00[\\x80-\\xff]A">::matches(bytes));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
assert(StaticRegex!"^(ab|a)+[0-9]?$".matches("aba7"));
ubyte[] bytes = [0, 255, 65];
assert(StaticRegex!`\x00[\x80-\xff]A`.matches(bytes));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s} requires the entire input. \texttt{p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} returns an optional longest matching end offset. \texttt{s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} returns the earliest matching \texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h}, choosing its longest end at that start. Match records have \texttt{b\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n}, \texttt{e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d}, and \texttt{s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}. C++ uses \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l}; D uses \texttt{N\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e}. Absolute \texttt{\textasciicircum{}}/\allowbreak{}\texttt{\$} assertions still refer to the full input when \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}t} is nonzero.

The existing \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n} and PEG \texttt{C\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n} APIs have their own smaller syntax and matching semantics. Static byte regexes provide the grouping, alternation and binary-stream matching facility documented here.

\section{Binary stream operations}

\texttt{B\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m} owns \texttt{b\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s} and a read cursor \texttt{p\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}s}. C++ accepts an input span; D accepts a \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}]}; each constructor copies the input. \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} returns a const view and advances the cursor, raising an exception on EOF. \texttt{w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} appends to storage rather than writing at the cursor.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
dsl::BinaryStream stream(bytes);
auto token = stream.next<"\\0[\\x80-\\xff]">();
assert(token && token->size() == 2);
assert(stream.pos == 2);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto stream = BinaryStream(bytes);
auto token = stream.next!`\0[\x80-\xff]`();
assert(token.get.length == 2 && stream.pos == 2);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\texttt{n\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}t} runs a static regex at the current cursor. Failure returns an empty optional and leaves \texttt{p\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}s} unchanged. A successful zero-length token raises an exception. Returned views refer to the stream's storage; storage-changing operations can invalidate views in C++. D slices retain their referenced allocation, but callers should obtain fresh views after changing storage.

Unsigned \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}>} /\allowbreak{} \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}T} and \texttt{w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{})} use \texttt{B\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}g} in C++ or \texttt{B\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}g} in D. The default is little-endian; each operation consumes/\allowbreak{}appends the type's byte width.

\texttt{B\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} repeatedly reads through abstract I/\allowbreak{}O until EOF. Its default limit is 64 MiB. Exceeding the configured limit raises an exception. \texttt{s\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{})} writes the whole buffer through \texttt{w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}l}, regardless of \texttt{p\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}s}. The user codec can compose these operations into its own file/\allowbreak{}record protocol.
